            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Remove about {{convert|1|in|cm}} of wire covering from each end of 2 copper wires. You should use 2 different colored copper wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut {{convert|1|in|cm}} of the plastic insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set up the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit. Plug the wire leads into the power pack.[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Adjust the voltage. You can turn a dial on your power pack to cause the voltage to fluctuate. Doing this can demonstrate how the brightness of the light changes as a result of higher or lower voltages. The light should get dimmer as the voltage goes down, and brighter as the voltage goes up.[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Remove about {{convert|1|in|cm}} of wire covering from each end of 2 copper wires. You should use 2 different colored copper wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut {{convert|1|in|cm}} of the plastic insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set up the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit. Plug the wire leads into the power pack.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Adjust the voltage. You can turn a dial on your power pack to cause the voltage to fluctuate. Doing this can demonstrate how the brightness of the light changes as a result of higher or lower voltages. The light should get dimmer as the voltage goes down, and brighter as the voltage goes up.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
