            "id": "24404582",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "18:11, 18 April 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,955 bytes)",
            "change": 11,
            "summary": "(Incorporating expert feedback)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "XxVxX",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder. A bulb holder is a device made to hold a light bulb. It also has 2 terminals. One is for a positive lead and the other is for a negative lead. This allows you to pass a current through the light bulb in the holder.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Be sure to use a low powered light bulb (e.g., in the range of 1-10 volts).",
                    "#Remove about {{convert|1|in|cm}} of wire covering from each end of 2 copper wires. You should use 2 different colored copper wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut an {{convert|1|in|cm}} of the plastic insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* If you weren't able to get any red wires, then choose 1 of your 2 colors to be the positive wire.",
                    "#Set up the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit. Plug the wire leads into the power pack.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut 1 wire lead. Remove power from the circuit before cutting any leads. It does not matter if you cut the positive or negative lead. You can use a pair of wire cutters to cut the lead anywhere in the circuit. The switch will provide control of the circuit no matter where it is located.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach the lead wire from the battery to the switch. Once you have cut 1 of the lead wires, you can attach it to the switch. The switch will have 2 simple terminals. Attach the lead wire coming from the battery to one of these terminals.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "*Use adult supervision to complete this project.",
                    "*Do not use more than 9-12 volts (DC) or a mild shock is possible (although AC is far more dangerous than DC).",
                    "*A 9-volt battery",
                    "__PARTS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder. A bulb holder is a device made to hold a light bulb. It also has two terminals. One is for a positive lead and the other is for a negative lead. This allows you to pass a current through the light bulb in the holder.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Be sure to use a low powered light bulb (e.g. something in the range of 1-10 volts).",
                    "#Remove about {{convert|1|in|cm}} of wire covering from each end of two copper wires. You should use two different colored copper wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut an {{convert|1|in|cm}} of the plastic insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* If you weren't able to get any red wires, then choose one of your two colors to be the positive wire.",
                    "#Set the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit. Plug the wire leads into the power pack.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut one wire lead. Remove power from the circuit before cutting any leads. It does not matter if you cut the positive or negative lead. You can use a pair of wire cutters to cut the lead anywhere in the circuit. The switch will provide control of the circuit no matter where it is located.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach the lead wire from the battery to the switch. Once you have cut one of the lead wires, you can attach it to the switch. The switch will have two simple terminals. Attach the lead wire coming from the battery to one of these terminals.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
