        {
            "id": "22282428",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "14:11, 24 April 2017",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,261 bytes)",
            "change": -1464,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder. A bulb holder is a device made to hold a light bulb. It also has two terminals. One is for a positive lead and the other is for a negative lead. This allows you to pass a current through the light bulb in the holder. Be sure to use a low powered light bulb (e.g. something in the range of 1-10 volts).<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of two copper wires. You should use two different colored copper wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut an inch of the wire insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the positive lead. Generally, red wire is used to connect the positive end. One side of the red wire will connect to one side of the bulb holder. The other side of the red wire should touch the positive lead on the battery.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the negative lead. Black wire is usually used to connect the negative end. Again, one end of the wire should touch the terminal on the bulb holder (not the same terminal as the positive wire). The other end of the wire can be left unattached until you are ready to light the bulb.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Light the bulb. Touch the unattached end of the negative (black) wire to the negative terminal on the battery. This completes the circuit and allows electricity to flow. The electricity is forced to flow through the light bulb, which causes the bulb to light up.[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit. Plug the wire leads into the power pack.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the light. Screw the light into a bulb holder. Then connect each lead from the power pack to one of the terminals on the bulb holder. Once both leads are connected, the light will light up.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Adjust the voltage. You can turn a dial on your power pack to cause the voltage to fluctuate. Doing this can demonstrate how the brightness of the light changes as a result of higher or lower voltages. The light should get dimmer as the voltage goes down, and brighter as the voltage goes up.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut one wire lead. Remove power from the circuit before cutting any leads. It does not matter if you cut the positive or negative lead. You can use a pair of wire cutters to cut the lead anywhere in the circuit. The switch will provide control of the circuit no matter where it is located.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach the lead wire from the battery to the switch. Once you have cut one of the lead wires, you can attach it to the switch. The switch will have two simple terminals. Attach the lead wire coming from the battery to one of these terminals.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach the lead wire from the switch to the bulb. The second piece of wire should be attached to the bulb holder terminal. Attach this piece of wire to the second terminal on the switch. This will again complete the circuit.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Toggle the switch. As you flip the switch on and off, it will open (break) and close (complete) the circuit. This will either prevent or allow electricity to flow. When the circuit is open, the light will be off. When the circuit is closed, the light will turn on.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 12.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder. A bulb holder is a device made to hold a light bulb. It also has two terminals. One is for a positive lead and the other is for a negative lead. This allows you to pass a current through the light bulb in the holder. Be sure to use a low powered light bulb (e.g. something in the range of 1-10 volts).<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 2-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of two copper wires. You should use two different colored copper wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut an inch of the wire insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 3.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect the positive lead. Generally, red wire is used to connect the positive end. One side of the red wire will connect to one side of the bulb holder. The other side of the red wire should touch the positive lead on the battery.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 4-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 4.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect the negative lead. Black wire is usually used to connect the negative end. Again, one end of the wire should touch the terminal on the bulb holder (not the same terminal as the positive wire). The other end of the wire can be left unattached until you are ready to light the bulb.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 5-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 5.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Light the bulb. Touch the unattached end of the negative (black) wire to the negative terminal on the battery. This completes the circuit and allows electricity to flow. The electricity is forced to flow through the light bulb, which causes the bulb to light up.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 6.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Set the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit. Plug the wire leads into the power pack.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 8.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect the light. Screw the light into a bulb holder. Then connect each lead from the power pack to one of the terminals on the bulb holder. Once both leads are connected, the light will light up.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 9.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Adjust the voltage. You can turn a dial on your power pack to cause the voltage to fluctuate. Doing this can demonstrate how the brightness of the light changes as a result of higher or lower voltages. The light should get dimmer as the voltage goes down, and brighter as the voltage goes up.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 10-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Cut one wire lead. Remove power from the circuit before cutting any leads. It does not matter if you cut the positive or negative lead. You can use a pair of wire cutters to cut the lead anywhere in the circuit. The switch will provide control of the circuit no matter where it is located.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>",
                    "#Attach the lead wire from the battery to the switch. Once you have cut one of the lead wires, you can attach it to the switch. The switch will have two simple terminals. Attach the lead wire coming from the battery to one of these terminals.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>",
                    "#Attach the lead wire from the switch to the bulb. The second piece of wire should be attached to the bulb holder terminal. Attach this piece of wire to the second terminal on the switch. This will again complete the circuit.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>",
                    "#Toggle the switch. As you flip the switch on and off, it will open (break) and close (complete) the circuit. This will either prevent or allow electricity to flow. When the circuit is open, the light will be off. When the circuit is closed, the light will turn on.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 11-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 11.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "22149883",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "03:48, 31 March 2017",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,725 bytes)",
            "change": 445,
            "summary": "",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Circuits allow electricity to flow in a circular path from a positive to a negative lead. Simple circuits make a great tool for teaching basic electrical concepts, and for experimenting with electricity at home. Building a circuit is not hard as long as you have a power source, some wires, and a light bulb (or other electrical component). If you want to learn even more about circuits, you can attach a simple switch that allows you to turn the bulb on and off. While this isn’t necessary, it does demonstrate open and closed circuits nicely.",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder. A bulb holder is a device made to hold a light bulb. It also has two terminals. One is for a positive lead and the other is for a negative lead. This allows you to pass a current through the light bulb in the holder. Be sure to use a low powered light bulb (e.g. something in the range of 1-10 volts).<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 2-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of two copper wires. You should use two different colored copper wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut an inch of the wire insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 3.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Set the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit. Plug the wire leads into the power pack.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 8.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Cut one wire lead. Remove power from the circuit before cutting any leads. It does not matter if you cut the positive or negative lead. You can use a pair of wire cutters to cut the lead anywhere in the circuit. The switch will provide control of the circuit no matter where it is located.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Circuits allow electricity to flow in a circular path from a positive to a negative lead. Simple circuits make a great tool for teaching basic electrical concepts, and for experimenting with electricity at home. Building a circuit is not hard as long as you have a power source, some wires, and a light bulb (or other electrical component).",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder. A bulb holder is a device made to hold a light bulb. It also has two terminals. One is for a positive lead and the other is for a negative lead. This allows you to pass a current through the light bulb in the holder.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 2-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of two wires. You should use two different colored wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut an inch of the wire insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 3.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Set the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 8.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Cut one lead. Remove power from the circuit before cutting any leads. It does not matter if you cut the positive or negative lead. The switch will provide control of ther circuit either way.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "22146206",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "14:45, 30 March 2017",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,280 bytes)",
            "change": 2320,
            "summary": "(Expanded Methods/Sources/Accuracy/Detail/Relevance/Edited Grammar)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Circuits allow electricity to flow in a circular path from a positive to a negative lead. Simple circuits make a great tool for teaching basic electrical concepts, and for experimenting with electricity at home. Building a circuit is not hard as long as you have a power source, some wires, and a light bulb (or other electrical component).",
                    "==Steps==",
                    "===Using a Battery===",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder. A bulb holder is a device made to hold a light bulb. It also has two terminals. One is for a positive lead and the other is for a negative lead. This allows you to pass a current through the light bulb in the holder.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 2-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of two wires. You should use two different colored wires. This will help you differentiate between the positive and negative lead. Use a knife or wire strippers to cut an inch of the wire insulation (the colored part) off of each end of both wires. This exposes the copper wire underneath.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 3.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect the positive lead. Generally, red wire is used to connect the positive end. One side of the red wire will connect to one side of the bulb holder. The other side of the red wire should touch the positive lead on the battery.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 4-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 4.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect the negative lead. Black wire is usually used to connect the negative end. Again, one end of the wire should touch the terminal on the bulb holder (not the same terminal as the positive wire). The other end of the wire can be left unattached until you are ready to light the bulb.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 5-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 5.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Light the bulb. Touch the unattached end of the negative (black) wire to the negative terminal on the battery. This completes the circuit and allows electricity to flow. The electricity is forced to flow through the light bulb, which causes the bulb to light up.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 6.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "===Using a Power Pack===",
                    "#Set the power pack. The power pack should be on a flat, level surface. Plug the power pack into an outlet. This will provide a steady supply of power to your circuit.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 8.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect the light. Screw the light into a bulb holder. Then connect each lead from the power pack to one of the terminals on the bulb holder. Once both leads are connected, the light will light up.<ref>https://www.homesciencetools.com/a/circuit-science-projects-for-elementary</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 9.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*If the light does not light up, check that the leads are well connected and the power pack is plugged in and turned on.",
                    "#Adjust the voltage. You can turn a dial on your power pack to cause the voltage to fluctuate. Doing this can demonstrate how the brightness of the light changes as a result of higher or lower voltages. The light should get dimmer as the voltage goes down, and brighter as the voltage goes up.<ref>http://www.energizer.com/science-center/make-a-simple-circuit</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 10-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "===Adding a Switch===",
                    "#Cut one lead. Remove power from the circuit before cutting any leads. It does not matter if you cut the positive or negative lead. The switch will provide control of ther circuit either way.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>",
                    "#*It is dangerous to cut into a live wire (one with power on it). Always disconnect the circuit before cutting leads.",
                    "#Attach the lead wire from the battery to the switch. Once you have cut one of the lead wires, you can attach it to the switch. The switch will have two simple terminals. Attach the lead wire coming from the battery to one of these terminals.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>",
                    "#Attach the lead wire from the switch to the bulb. The second piece of wire should be attached to the bulb holder terminal. Attach this piece of wire to the second terminal on the switch. This will again complete the circuit.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref>",
                    "#Toggle the switch. As you flip the switch on and off, it will open (break) and close (complete) the circuit. This will either prevent or allow electricity to flow. When the circuit is open, the light will be off. When the circuit is closed, the light will turn on.<ref>https://www.allaboutcircuits.com/textbook/experiments/chpt-2/circuit-with-a-switch/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 11-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 11.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "==Video==",
                    "==Warnings==",
                    "==Things You'll Need==",
                    "*A 9 volt battery",
                    "*A switch",
                    "==Related wikiHows==",
                    "==Sources and Citations==",
                    "{{reflist}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "{{stub|date=2016-05-12}}If you're looking for some helpful advice to make a working light bulb then you've come to the right place. Scroll down and let's get started!",
                    "",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "===  Using Bulbs and Other Equipment ===",
                    "#Get a 10-volt bulb, 9-volt battery and 2 wires. Using two wires of different colors makes this process easier.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 1 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 1-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 1.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 1 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 1 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 2-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of the wires with a wire stripper or a pair of scissors.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 3.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect one end of a wire to the positive end of a 9-volt battery and the other end to the center of the bulb holder. Generally, red is used to connect the positive end.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 4-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 4.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 9-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder. Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 5-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 5.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Now your bulb will light up!<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 6.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "===  Using a Power Pack ===",
                    "#You can also use a power pack to make a circuit. To make a circuit in this way, You need to do the following:<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 7 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 7-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 7.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 7 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 7 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Place the power pack on the desk, ensuring that it is connected to the main supply.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 8.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Collect all equipment needed, wires and bulbs, you do not need a battery ensuring that there are no breaks in the circuit, (breaks mean that there is a gap in the circuit)<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 9.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Adjust the voltage by turning a dial on the power pack; this will give you varying degrees of light.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 10-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "===  Adding a Switch ===",
                    "#You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the light bulb, connect the light bulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 11-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 11.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "== Video ==",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "* Make sure it is firmly connected.",
                    "*Double check the wiring if you are having problems.",
                    "* Make sure it doesn't burn out.",
                    "*Use one battery for 9 volts power.",
                    "* Some battery holders have 9v snaps.",
                    "* Make sure you are using the right kind of batteries.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "*A 9 volts battery",
                    "",
                    "== Related wikiHows ==",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
