                    "#Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 9-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder. Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 5-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 5.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Now your bulb will light up!<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 6.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#You can also use a power pack to make a circuit. To make a circuit in this way, You need to do the following:<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 7 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 7-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 7.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 7 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 7 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Place the power pack on the desk, ensuring that it is connected to the main supply.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 8.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Collect all equipment needed, wires and bulbs, you do not need a battery ensuring that there are no breaks in the circuit, (breaks mean that there is a gap in the circuit)<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 9.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Adjust the voltage by turning a dial on the power pack; this will give you varying degrees of light.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 10-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the light bulb, connect the light bulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 11-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 11.jpg|gif=Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Get a 10-volt bulb, 9-volt battery and 2 wires. Using two wires of different colors makes this process easier.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 1 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 1-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 1.jpg}}",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 2 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 2-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of the wires with a wire stripper or a pair of scissors.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Connect one end of a wire to the positive end of a 9-volt battery and the other end to the center of the bulb holder. Generally, red is used to connect the positive end.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 4 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 4-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 4.jpg}}",
                    "#Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 9-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder. Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 5 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 5-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 5.jpg}}",
                    "#Now your bulb will light up!<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 6.jpg}}",
                    "#You can also use a power pack to make a circuit. To make a circuit in this way, You need to do the following:<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 7 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 7-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 7.jpg}}",
                    "#Place the power pack on the desk, ensuring that it is connected to the main supply.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 8.jpg}}",
                    "#Collect all equipment needed, wires and bulbs, you do not need a battery ensuring that there are no breaks in the circuit, (breaks mean that there is a gap in the circuit)<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 9.jpg}}",
                    "#Adjust the voltage by turning a dial on the power pack; this will give you varying degrees of light.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 10-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 10 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the light bulb, connect the light bulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery.<br><br>{{whvid|Make a Circuit Step 11 Version 2.360p.mp4|Make a Circuit Step 11-preview Version 2.jpg|Make a Circuit Step 11.jpg}}"
                ]
