        {
            "id": "11625724",
            "user": "Jordan",
            "timestamp": "14:46, 31 October 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(3,444 bytes)",
            "change": 3396,
            "summary": "(Reverted edits by 90.245.12.153 (Talk); changed back to last version by 70.161.93.146)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "reverted_by": "Rock20000",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "If you're looking for some helpful advice to make a working light bulb then you've come to the right place. Scroll down and let's get started!",
                    "[[Category:Electrical Maintenance]]",
                    "",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "=== Method 1: Using Bulbs and Other Equipment ===",
                    "#Get a 10-volt bulb, 9-volt battery and 2 wires. Using two wires of different colors makes this process easier.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 1.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 2.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of the wires with a wire stripper or a pair of scissors.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 3.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect one end of a wire to the positive end of a 9-volt battery and the other end to the center of the bulb holder. Generally, red is used to connect the positive end.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 4.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 9-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder. Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 5.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Now your bulb will light up!<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 6.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "=== Method 2: Using a Power Pack ===",
                    "#You can also use a power pack to make a circuit. To make a circuit in this way, You need to do the following:<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 7.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Place the power pack on the desk, ensuring that it is connected to the main supply.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 8.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Collect all equipment needed, wires and bulbs, you do not need a battery ensuring that there are no breaks in the circuit, (breaks mean that there is a gap in the circuit)<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 9.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Adjust the voltage by turning a dial on the power pack; this will give you varying degrees of light.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "=== Method 3: Adding a Switch ===",
                    "#You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the light bulb, connect the light bulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 11.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "",
                    "== Video ==",
                    "{{Video:Make a Circuit|}}",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "*Double check the wiring if you are having problems.",
                    "* Some battery holders have 9v snaps.",
                    "* Make sure it doesn't burn out.",
                    "*Use one battery for 9 volts power.",
                    "* Make sure it is firmly connected.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "*Do not use more than nine volts or a mild shock is possible.",
                    "*The light bulb can become hot, do not touch it while it is on.",
                    "",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "*Light bulb",
                    "*Light bulb holder",
                    "*2 wires of different colors (copper works best)",
                    "*A 9 volts battery",
                    "",
                    "== Related wikiHows ==",
                    "*[[Install an Electrical Outlet from Scratch|How to Install an Electrical Outlet from Scratch]]",
                    "*[[Make a Voltage Divider Circuit|How to Make a Voltage Divider Circuit]]",
                    "*[[Do Common Electrical Repairs|How to Do Common Electrical Repairs]]",
                    "*[[Change a Stuck Lightbulb|How to Change a Stuck Lightbulb]]",
                    "*[[Make a Simple Electrical Circuit|How to Make a Simple Electrical Circuit]]",
                    "*[[Build a Parallel Circuit|How to Build a Parallel Circuit]]",
                    "",
                    "[[es:hacer un circuito]]",
                    "[[de:Einen Stromkreis herstellen]]",
                    "[[it:Fare un Circuito]]",
                    "[[pt:Fazer um Circuito]]",
                    "[[fr:faire un circuit électrique]]",
                    "[[nl:Een stroomkring maken]]",
                    "[[ru:построить электрическую сеть]]",
                    "[[zh:连电路]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "poo faaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaacccccccccccccccceeeeeeeeee"
                ]
            }
        },
