            "id": "8919878",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "18:26, 24 December 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(3,133 bytes)",
            "change": 100,
            "summary": "(Enlarging and centering Steps photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Get a 10-volt bulb, bulb holder, 9-volt battery and 2 wires. Using two wires of different colors makes this process easier.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 1.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 2.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of the wires with a wire stripper or a pair of scissors.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 3.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect one end of a wire to the positive end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the center of the bulb holder. Generally, red is used to connect the positive end.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 4.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder. Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 5.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Now your bulb will light up!<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 6.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#You can also use a power pack to make a circuit. To make a circuit in this way, You need to do the following:<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 7.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Place the power pack on the desk, ensuring that it is connected to the main supply.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 8.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Collect all equipment needed, wires and bulbs, you do not need a battery ensuring that there are no breaks in the circuit, (breaks mean that there is a gap in the circuit)<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 9.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Adjust the voltage by turning a dial on the power pack; this will give you varying degrees of light.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the light bulb, connect the light bulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery.<br><br>[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 11.jpeg|center|550px]]",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 1.jpeg|right|300px]]Get a 10-volt bulb, bulb holder, 9-volt battery and 2 wires. Using two wires of different colors makes this process easier.",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 2.jpeg|right|300px]]Screw a bulb into a bulb holder.",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 3.jpeg|right|300px]]Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of the wires with a wire stripper or a pair of scissors.",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 4.jpeg|right|300px]]Connect one end of a wire to the positive end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the center of the bulb holder. Generally, red is used to connect the positive end.",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 5.jpeg|right|300px]]Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder. Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 6.jpeg|right|300px]]Now your bulb will light up!",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 7.jpeg|right|300px]]You can also use a power pack to make a circuit. To make a circuit in this way, You need to do the following:",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 8.jpeg|right|300px]]Place the power pack on the desk, ensuring that it is connected to the main supply.",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 9.jpeg|right|300px]]Collect all equipment needed, wires and bulbs, you do not need a battery ensuring that there are no breaks in the circuit, (breaks mean that there is a gap in the circuit)",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 10.jpg|right|300px]]Adjust the voltage by turning a dial on the power pack; this will give you varying degrees of light.",
                    "#[[Image:Make a Circuit Step 11.jpeg|right|300px]]You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the light bulb, connect the light bulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery."
                ]
            }
        },
