                    "== Steps ==",
                    "=== Method 1 ===",
                    "#Get a 10-volt bulb, bulb holder, 9-volt battery and 2 wires. Using two wires of different colors makes this process easier.",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder.",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of the wires with a wire stripper or a pair of scissors.",
                    "#[[Image:Circuit Illustration.jpg|right|225px]]'''Connect one end of a wire to the positive end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the center of the bulb holder.''' Generally, red is used to connect the positive end.",
                    "#'''Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder.''' Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.",
                    "#Now your bulb will light up!",
                    "=== Method 2 ===",
                    "#You can also use a power pack to make a circuit. To make a circuit in this way, You need to do the following:",
                    "#Place the power pack on the desk, ensuring that it is connected to the main supply.",
                    "#Collect all equipment needed, wires and bulbs, you do not need a battery for this circuit.",
                    "#Connect the circuit together, ensuring that there are know breaks in the circuit, (breaks mean that there is a gap in the circuit)",
                    "#Adjust the voltage by turning a dial on the power pack; this will give you varying degrees of light.",
                    "=== Optional Tweaks ===",
                    "#You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the light bulb, connect the light bulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery."
