        {
            "id": "6070627",
            "user": "WikiHowUser20221012045502",
            "timestamp": "20:35, 25 May 2011",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(2,411 bytes)",
            "change": -1849,
            "summary": "(Revert to best edit)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "[[Image:Labyrinthine circuit board lines 4.jpg|{{BASEPAGENAME}}|right|251px]]Want to light up your own lightbulb? With this simple circuit, it's easy!",
                    "=== Method 2 ===",
                    "You can also use a powerpack to make a circuit. To make a circuit in this way, You need to do the following:",
                    "#Place the powerpack on the desk, ensuring that it is connected to the main supply.",
                    "#Collect all equipment needed, wires and bulbs, you do not need a battery for this circuit.",
                    "#Connect the circuit together, ensuring that there are know breaks in the circuit, (breaks mean that there is a gap in the circuit)",
                    "#Adjust the voltage by turning a dial on the powerpack; this will give you varying degrees of light.",
                    "== Video ==",
                    "{{Video:Make a Circuit|}}",
                    "== Related wikiHows ==",
                    "*[[Install an Electrical Outlet from Scratch|How to Install an Electrical Outlet from Scratch]]",
                    "*[[Make a Voltage Divider Circuit|How to Make a Voltage Divider Circuit]]",
                    "*[[Do Common Electrical Repairs|How to Do Common Electrical Repairs]]",
                    "*[[Change a Stuck Lightbulb|How to Change a Stuck Lightbulb]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Want to light up your own lightbulb? With this simple circuit, it's easy!",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "=== Optional Tweaks ===",
                    "",
                    "You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the lightbulb, connect the lightbulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery.Want to light up your own lightbulb? With this simple circuit, it's easy!",
                    "",
                    "[[Category:Electronics]]",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "=== Method 1 ===",
                    "#Get a 10-volt bulb, bulb holder, 9-volt battery and 2 wires. Using two wires of different colors makes this process easier.",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder.",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of the wires with a wire stripper or a pair of scissors.",
                    "#[[Image:Circuit Illustration.jpg|right|225px]]'''Connect one end of a wire to the positive end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the center of the bulb holder.''' Generally, red is used to connect the positive end.",
                    "#'''Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder.''' Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.",
                    "#Now your bulb will light up!",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "*Use one more battery for 3 volts power (bulb will light more brightly).",
                    "*Double check the wiring if you are having problems.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==Want to light up your own lightbulb? With this simple circuit, it's easy!",
                    "",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "=== Method 1 ===",
                    "#Get a 10-volt bulb, bulb holder, 9-volt battery and 2 wires. Using two wires of different colors makes this process easier.",
                    "#Screw a bulb into a bulb holder.",
                    "#Remove about an inch of wire covering from each end of the wires with a wire stripper or a pair of scissors.",
                    "#[[Image:Circuit Illustration.jpg|right|225px]]'''Connect one end of a wire to the positive end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the center of the bulb holder.''' Generally, red is used to connect the positive end.",
                    "#'''Connect the other wire to the negative end of a 1.5-volt battery and the other end to the side of the bulb holder.''' Generally, black is used to connect the negative end.",
                    "#Now your bulb will light up!",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "=== Optional Tweaks ===",
                    "",
                    "You can use a switch to turn the circuit on/off.  Instead of connecting the battery to the lightbulb, connect the lightbulb to the switch, then the switch to the battery.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "*Don't use more than 3 volts or you might get shocked really hard.",
                    "",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "*Light bulb",
                    "*Light bulb holder",
                    "*2 wires of different colors (copper works best)",
                    "*A 1.5-volt battery",
                    "*Battery holder (optional)",
                    "*Switch (optional)",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "*Use one more battery for 3 volts power (bulb will light more brightly).",
                    "*Double check the wiring if you are having problems.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "",
                    "*Don't use more than 3 volts or you might get shocked really hard.",
                    "",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "*Light bulb",
                    "*Light bulb holder",
                    "*2 wires of different colors (copper works best)",
                    "*A 1.5-volt battery",
                    "*Battery holder (optional)",
                    "*Switch (optional)"
                ]
            }
        },
